3. CREATE THE LOADING STATE
================================================================================

Create state inside App:

const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);


Explanation:

---------------------------+----------------------------------------------
Code                       | Purpose
---------------------------+----------------------------------------------
isLoading                  | Current loading status
setIsLoading()             | Updates loading status
useState(false)            | Initial loading value
setIsLoading(true)         | Start loading
setIsLoading(false)        | Stop loading
---------------------------+----------------------------------------------


================================================================================
4. START LOADING BEFORE THE API CALL
================================================================================

Example:

const fetchBooks = async (query) => {

  setIsLoading(true);

  const response = await fetch(URL);

};


What Happens:

- fetchBooks() starts.
- setIsLoading(true) requests a state update.
- React re-renders.
- Loading UI becomes visible.
- API request continues in the background.


Simple Flow:

fetchBooks()
     |
     v
setIsLoading(true)
     |
     v
React Re-renders
     |
     v
Show Loading
     |
     v
fetch(URL)


================================================================================
5. STOP LOADING AFTER THE API CALL
================================================================================

Basic Example:

const fetchBooks = async (query) => {

  setIsLoading(true);

  const response = await fetch(URL);

  const data = await response.json();

  setBooks(data.items || []);

  setIsLoading(false);

};


Explanation:

- Loading starts before fetch().
- API request completes.
- Books are stored in state.
- Loading is changed back to false.
- React re-renders with book results.


================================================================================
6. PROBLEM WITH THE BASIC APPROACH
================================================================================

Consider:

const fetchBooks = async () => {

  setIsLoading(true);

  const response = await fetch(URL);

  const data = await response.json();

  setBooks(data.items || []);

  setIsLoading(false);

};


Problem:

If fetch() throws an error, execution may never reach:

setIsLoading(false);


Possible result:

isLoading remains true

and the screen keeps showing:

Loading books...


Therefore, loading must stop on both:

- Success
- Failure


================================================================================
7. USE try / catch / finally
================================================================================

Recommended:

const fetchBooks = async (query) => {

  try {

    setIsLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    const URL =
      `https://www.googleapis.com/books/v1/volumes?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`;

    const response = await fetch(URL);

    if (!response.ok) {

      throw new Error(
        `HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`
      );

    }

    const data = await response.json();

    setBooks(data.items || []);

  } catch (error) {

    setError(error.message);
    setBooks([]);

  } finally {

    setIsLoading(false);

  }

};


WHAT THIS CODE DOES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

try:

- Starts loading.
- Clears previous errors.
- Calls the API.
- Reads the response.
- Updates books.


catch:

- Executes when an error occurs.
- Stores the error message.
- Clears the books if appropriate.


finally:

- Executes after try/catch.
- Runs whether the operation succeeds or fails.
- Stops the loading state.


================================================================================
8. WHY finally IS IMPORTANT
================================================================================

Without finally:

try {

  ...

  setIsLoading(false);

} catch (error) {

  ...

  setIsLoading(false);

}


Problem:

setIsLoading(false)

is duplicated.


Better:

try {

  ...

} catch (error) {

  ...

} finally {

  setIsLoading(false);

}


Benefits:

- Less duplicate code
- Easier to maintain
- Loading reliably stops
- Works for success and failure


